<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Athena</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Athena was the ancient Greek goddess of wisdom, crafts and war, the virgin daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, said to have sprung fully armed from his head. Patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> was her temple, she was worshipped in sanctuaries across the Greek world, at Lindos, Smyrna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> among others, often as protector of the citadel. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athena.jpg" alt="Athena" width="320" height="640" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jastrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mattei_Athena_Louvre_Ma530_n2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Athena, often called Pallas, was probably an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> goddess of the palace, who presided over household crafts and protected the king. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> reads a-ta-na po-ti-ni-ja, which may mean Mistress Athena or the Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, although any link to the city is uncertain. Scholars now generally agree that the goddess takes her name from the city, not the reverse. Similar city goddesses took their names from their cities elsewhere, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mykene</a> at Mycenae and Thebe at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p><p>Her epithets show her roles. Polias and Poliouchos mean of the city, Ergane the industrious, Parthenos the virgin, and Promachos she who fights in front. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s most common epithet for her is Glaukopis, usually translated bright-eyed. The little owl was associated with her from early on and became the mascot of the Athenians and later a symbol of wisdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the classical Olympian pantheon Athena was the favourite child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, born fully armed from his forehead. As goddess of war she was the divine form of the heroic ideal, represented as the intellectual and civilised side of war, in contrast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>, who stood for mere bloodlust. She aided the heroes Perseus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellerophon">Bellerophon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> she is the tutelary deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>.</p><p>In a founding myth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, reported by Pseudo-Apollodorus, she competed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> for the city's patronage. Poseidon struck the ground and raised a salt spring, and Athena offered the first olive tree. Cecrops, the king of Athens, judged hers the better gift. She was also one of the goddesses whose quarrel over a golden apple, settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, led her to side with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> her temples stood on the Acropolis, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> was dedicated to her. Her main festival, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaia</a>, was held in the month of Hekatombaion in midsummer and was the most important on the Athenian calendar. As Athena Ergane she was patron of crafts, especially weaving and metalwork, and in the late fifth century BCE her role as goddess of philosophy became a major part of her cult.</p><p>She was later syncretised with the Roman goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> and the Etruscan Menrva. Early Christian writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-alexandria">Clement of Alexandria</a> condemned her, but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> some of her attributes passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, and she was used as a Christian allegory. Since the Renaissance Athena has been an international symbol of wisdom, the arts and classical learning. Her cult is revived today in Hellenismos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Athena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rich silver mines of Laurion supplied the tetradrachms stamped with <b>Athena</b>'s owl, among the most trusted coins of the Greek world, and a new vein found in 483 BCE paid for the fleet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Boeotians shared an Aeolic dialect, a tradition of common descent and cults such as that of <b>Athena</b> Itonia near Coronea and Poseidon at Onchestus, which gave a basis for joint action.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings worshipped the Greek pantheon shown on their coins, Zeus, Herakles, <b>Athena</b> and Apollo, but also took part in Indian religions: Heliodorus, ambassador of Antialcidas, called himself a devotee of Vāsudeva on his pillar at Vidisha around 113 BCE, the first known inscription of the Bhagavata movement,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On the mainland the Mycenaean Greeks recorded offerings to their gods on clay tablets in the Linear B script, deciphered in 1952: among the names are Zeus, Hera, Poseidon, Hermes, Artemis and Dionysus, and a “Mistress of Athana” who may be an early <b>Athena</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 6th century BCE many of them were identified with Greek gods, Tinia with Zeus, Uni with Hera, Menrva with <b>Athena</b>, Fufluns with Dionysus, and bronze mirrors engraved with Greek myths label the figures with Etruscan names.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The most frequent title is Potnia, “Lady” or “Mistress”, given to one or more goddesses, as in the “Potnia of the labyrinth” at Knossos and the “Potnia of the horses” at Pylos, and a “Potnia of Atana” at Knossos may be an early form of <b>Athena</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
